# CHAPTER 6: "PREPARATION"

---

Morning light cut through the blinds in sharp lines. Kai packed his gear quietly—pistol, spare mags, combat knife, light armor plates that fit under his jacket. Methodical. Professional. Trying not to think about what came next.

Caldera. The desert. Close to Ashen Reach.

Close to ghosts he'd buried.

"Kai?"

He turned. Mira stood in the doorway, still in pajamas, rubbing sleep from her eyes.

"Hey, kid. You're up early."

"You're leaving." Not a question. She always knew.

"Yeah. Work. Be back tonight."

She crossed the room. Hugged him. He hugged back, careful not to let her feel the armor under his shirt.

"Be safe."

"Always am."

She pulled back. Looked at him with those too-perceptive eyes. "You promise?"

He smiled. Lied. "I promise."

She believed him. Went back to her room.

Kai exhaled. Finished packing.

Stitch appeared in the doorway. Silent. Watching.

"Big job?" he asked quietly.

"Information retrieval. Caldera."

Stitch's expression darkened. "That's nomad territory. Lawless. Dangerous."

"I know."

"Does this have to do with the Surge?"

"Yeah. There's a corpo exec with data. Program files, testing logs, everything Atlas buried. Elena thinks it'll help us understand what I'm carrying."

Stitch pulled a small case from his pocket. Medical container. Inside: Three vials, clear liquid. "Neutralizers. Upgraded formula. If the Surge activates hard—if you feel it taking over—inject one. Might keep you conscious enough to stay in control."

Kai took the case. Pocketed it. "Might?"

"It's experimental. But it's better than nothing." Stitch's voice was quiet. Worried. "Come back, Kai. Mira needs you."

"I will."

"And if things go bad—if you lose control—"

"I won't."

Stitch didn't look convinced. Just nodded. "Good luck."

Kai shouldered his gear. Walked toward the door.

Stopped. Looked back.

Mira's drawings taped to the wall. Stick figures holding hands. FAMILY.

Stitch standing in the kitchen. Grey hair, tired eyes, father-figure who'd saved his life and damned his soul in the same stroke.

Home. Anchor. The reason he kept fighting.

"I'll be back," Kai said again. Meant it.

Then left.

---

Elena stood in her workshop, staring at the vehicle in the back storage bay.

Dust covered it. Months of neglect. But underneath—muscle. Power. Speed.

Dirt racing car. Lightweight carbon frame, reinforced roll cage, turbocharged engine that could hit 200kph on straightaways. Custom build. Black and chrome. Beautiful and brutal.

She hadn't touched it in eight months. Not since the last underground race. Not since she'd decided Diving paid better and hurt less.

But racing was in her blood. Always had been.

She pulled the dust cover off. Ran diagnostics via her Data Tap. Engine: Functional. Suspension: Optimal. Fuel cells: Charged.

Still ready. Like it had been waiting.

"You're racing?"

Elena turned. Ashira stood in the doorway. Silent as always. Observing.

"Used to. Underground circuits. Made good money before Diving paid better."

Ashira approached. Ran a hand along the car's frame. Respectful. Appreciating the craft. "You miss it."

"Sometimes. The speed. The focus. Everything else disappears." Elena opened the trunk. Started loading equipment. "But today it's just a tool. Fast enough to keep up with Cray's convoy. Maneuverable enough to corner him."

Ashira pulled her weapons from their case. Tech pistol—smart-targeting system, linked to her optics. Compact SMG—high rate of fire, low recoil, perfect for vehicle combat.

Elena noticed. "Didn't know you used guns."

"Blade is for honor. Guns are for survival."

"Fair." Elena loaded the last of her gear. Closed the trunk. "You ready for this?"

Ashira checked her weapons. Methodical. Professional. "Always."

Elena almost smiled. "Good. Because Caldera's going to be chaos."

---

Dax sat in his garage, hands covered in grease, tuning his combat sedan's engine.

Caldera. Going home.

Three years since he'd left. Three years since the ambush. Since his father died. Since his clan scattered like dust in the wind.

His Data Tap pinged. Message from Kai.

> Meet at staging point. 1100 hours. Abandoned industrial lot, coordinates attached.
> 

Dax wiped his hands. Stared at the nomad cord on his wrist. Each knot a life lost. Twelve knots. Twelve people who didn't make it out.

His father. His cousins. Friends he'd grown up with. All gone in one night of corporate violence.

And he'd survived. Drove away. Followed orders.

Lived.

The guilt never left. Just learned to carry it.

He stood. Grabbed his shotgun. Checked the load. Racked a shell.

"Going home," he muttered to the empty garage. "Finally."

Closed the garage door. Climbed into the sedan. Engine roared to life.

Drove toward the staging point. Toward Caldera. Toward ghosts.

---

The abandoned industrial lot sat on Veyline's outskirts—cracked asphalt, rusted shipping containers, weeds growing through concrete. Perfect for discreet meetings.

Kai arrived first. Parked his bike. Scanned the perimeter with Vantage Optics. Clear.

Waited.

Dax's combat sedan appeared ten minutes later. Engine purring smooth. Fresh modifications visible—armor plating on doors, reinforced bumper, weapon mounts concealed but present.

Dax stepped out. Saw Kai. Awkward moment—first time together since Stitch's clinic. Since Juno died. Since everything changed.

"You look better," Dax said. "Walking, at least."

"Thanks to Stitch. And you. You got me out."

"Barely. Should've been faster. Should've—"

Kai cut him off. "You saved my life. That's what matters."

Dax's jaw tightened. Wanted to argue. Didn't. Just nodded. "Yeah. Okay."

Brief moment of connection. Shared guilt, shared survival.

Then business.

"Big job?" Dax asked.

"Corpo exec. Caldera race. Has data we need."

"We?"

Another engine sound. Different pitch. Lighter, faster.

Elena's dirt racing car appeared—sleek, aggressive, built for speed. Rolled to a stop beside them.

Dax's eyebrows rose. "You race?"

Elena climbed out. "Used to. Still can."

Kai stared. "Didn't know that."

"There's a lot you don't know about me." Elena popped the trunk. Gear visible inside—tech equipment, portable servers, encryption tools.

Ashira appeared from the passenger seat. Moved like shadow. Unloaded her weapons—tech pistol, compact SMG.

Kai noticed. "Thought you only used the blade."

Ashira's response was brief. "Blade is for honor. Guns are for survival."

Dax laughed. Short. Genuine. "I like her."

Crew assembled. Four people. Two vehicles. One target.

Dax looked at Elena. Something he'd been wondering. "Can I ask you something?"

"Shoot."

"Why are you here? You're Elena Markovic. The Ghost. Solo operator. Biggest clients, highest bids. Why team up with us?"

Elena paused. Considered the question. Fair ask.

"Vera came to me six months ago. Said she was hunting something. Atlas Dynamics. Illegal R&D projects, black budget programs, tech that shouldn't exist."

She leaned against her car. "She needed a Diver. I needed... a purpose beyond money."

"Purpose?"

"I've spent years stealing corpo secrets. Selling to rivals. Getting rich. But it's all the same. They hide shit, I expose it, nothing changes." Elena's voice hardened. "Vera offered something different. Not just theft. Exposure. Shutting down programs that hurt people."

She looked at Kai. "Like the Surge. Like what's in your spine. That tech killed thirty-six people during testing. Atlas buried it. Sold the rest on black market. People are dying because corpos prioritize profit over lives."

"So yeah, I'm here. Because someone needs to drag this shit into the light."

Dax respected that. Nodded. "Okay. I'm in."

Kai's realization settled heavy. Elena wasn't just helping him. She was on a crusade. The Surge was one piece of something bigger.

Before he could respond, his Data Tap pinged. Incoming holo-call.

Vera.

He accepted. Holographic display materialized—Vera's face, sharp and professional.

"Everyone there?"

"Yeah," Kai confirmed.

"Good. Let's talk business."

---

Vera's hologram shifted. Displayed files, images, tactical data.

"Target: Damien Cray. Atlas Dynamics, R&D division, black budget operations. Mid-forties, adrenaline junkie, thinks money makes him untouchable."

Cray's face appeared. Corpo slick—expensive augments, tailored suit even in promotional photos, smug expression of someone who'd never faced consequences.

"He attends illegal street races in Caldera. Uses it as cover for off-books meetings, data exchanges, deals that can't happen in boardrooms."

Vera pulled up race intel. "Race happens today. 1400 hours. Desert circuit, thirty kilometers, no rules, high casualties. Cray races with a three-vehicle convoy. Security detail, heavy weapons, Atlas-trained drivers."

"What's he carrying?" Elena asked.

"Data. Full archive." Vera's expression was steel. "Not just Surge files. Everything. Atlas's entire black budget operation."

She expanded the list:

**PROJECT ARCHIVE CONTENTS:**

- Synaptic Combat Override (Mark VII) - Full documentation
- Neural control experiments
- Illegal augment testing logs
- Corporate assassination orders
- Blackout Division operational records
- Territorio War crimes evidence
- Embezzlement trails linking to Atlas board members

The crew went silent. This wasn't just one secret. This was an arsenal.

"This data goes public," Vera continued, "Atlas bleeds. Stock crashes. Operations shut down. Executives face charges. Maybe even prison."

Her voice dropped. Harder. "Juno died because Atlas wanted a corpo rat silenced. They've killed hundreds to protect these secrets. I want them bleeding. This is war."

Kai understood now. This wasn't just about him. About the Surge. This was Vera's revenge for her brother. Her way of making Atlas pay.

"The plan?" Dax asked.

"Two vehicles enter the race." Vera highlighted positions. "Elena and Ashira in the racing car. Dax and Kai in the combat sedan. You intercept Cray during the chaos, corner him, extract the data, escape before his security reacts."

"Payment?"

"Fifteen thousand creds each. Plus Elena gets a copy of the data for her archive."

Elena nodded. "Deal."

"One more thing," Vera said. "Caldera's lawless. No VSD, no backup, no rescue. If you're caught, you're on your own."

"Understood," Kai said.

Vera's hologram flickered. "Good hunting."

Disconnected.

The crew stood in silence. Processing.

Dax broke it. "So. Caldera. Anyone been?"

Elena: "Once. Five years ago. Nearly died."

Ashira: "Twice. Survived."

Kai: "Never. But I've heard stories."

Dax smiled. Grim. "Stories don't do it justice. It's beautiful and brutal. Just like home."

He checked his chronometer. "We should move. Two-hour drive. Need to be there by 1300 for race staging."

Crew loaded up. Weapons, gear, everything they'd need.

Kai climbed into Dax's sedan. Passenger seat. Familiar position.

Elena and Ashira took the racing car.

Engines started. Two-vehicle convoy. Heading southwest.

Toward Caldera. Toward answers. Toward danger.

Kai touched the neutralizers in his pocket. Prayed he wouldn't need them.

Knew he would.

---

The highway stretched endless. Veyline City faded in the rearview—neon and steel replaced by industrial waste and empty plains.

Sky opened up. No smog here. Just grey-blue expanse, clouds moving slow like they had nowhere to be.

Dax drove steady. One hand on wheel, other resting on the gearshift. Comfortable. This was his element.

But Kai noticed the tension. The way Dax's jaw tightened as they got closer. The way his eyes tracked landmarks—old signs, abandoned structures, places that meant something.

"You good?" Kai asked.

"Will be. Just... memories."

"We don't have to do this."

"Yeah, we do." Dax's voice was quiet. "I owe Juno. I owe you. And maybe... maybe I need to face it."

Kai didn't push. Just watched the desert roll by.

An hour passed. Then another.

Caldera appeared on the horizon.

Not a city. Not even a town. Just... settlement. Sprawling. Chaotic. Built from scraps and survival.

Vehicle graveyards stretched for kilometers—rusted frames, stripped parts, machines cannibalized for whatever still worked. Buildings were patchwork—shipping containers, prefab modules, welded metal sheets forming homes and shops.

Neon signs flickered. Repair shops. Fuel stations. Bars with names like "Last Stop" and "Dead End."

Nomad camps dotted the outskirts—tents, vehicles in circles, families living the way they'd lived for generations.

This was Caldera. Lawless. Brutal. Free.

Dax's home.

"Welcome to Caldera," he said quietly.

They drove deeper. The race staging area came into view—massive open lot, hundreds of vehicles, thousands of people.

Chaos incarnate.

---

The staging area was a circus of chrome and violence.

Racers tuned engines—roaring, revving, competing for loudest. Gangs marked territory with spray paint and body language. Betting rings formed—creds changing hands, odds shouted, fortunes won and lost before the race even started.

VSD was nowhere. Didn't patrol here. Couldn't. Caldera was beyond their jurisdiction, beyond their courage.

This was nomad law. Which meant no law at all.

Kai and Dax rolled in. Found parking in the staging rows. Elena and Ashira pulled up beside them.

Crew dismounted. Assessed.

"Holy shit," Elena muttered. "This is insane."

"This is Caldera," Dax said. Pride underneath the words. This was his people's world. Even if they'd cast him out.

Ashira scanned the crowd. Tactical assessment. Threats everywhere. "Stay close."

They moved as a unit. Kai's Vantage Optics tracked faces, weapons, exits. Old habits.

Elena ran final diagnostics on her racing car. "Systems green. Ready to roll."

Ashira loaded her tech weapons. Concealed but accessible.

Dax checked the combat sedan. Armor plating solid. Weapon mounts ready. Engine purring.

"Stay close during the race," he said to Kai. "If we get separated, rendezvous point is the north ridge. Three kilometers out."

"Copy."

Dax scanned the crowd. Looking for familiar faces. Saw nomad markings—Caldera Pack survivors, other clans. His chest tightened.

Then he saw him.

Kade. Across the staging area. Checking a vehicle—his own race car.

Dax tensed. Didn't call out. Just watched.

Kai noticed. "Someone you know?"

"Kade. The driver from the highway job. Didn't know he'd be here."

"Problem?"

"Don't know yet."

Before Dax could say more, Kai froze.

Saw someone else. Someone impossible.

"Silas?"

The man turned. Mid-thirties, worn face, survivor's eyes. Silas Ward. The corpo rat they'd tried to extract. The man who'd sacrificed himself so they could escape.

The man who was supposed to be dead.

Silas saw Kai. Expression shifted—surprise, then something harder.

Kai crossed the distance. "You're alive."

"Yeah. Surprise." Silas's voice was flat. Guarded.

"We thought—we thought you died. The distraction, the ambush—"

"I survived. Barely. Atlas wanted me dead. Still do." Silas looked past Kai. Saw Dax, Elena, Ashira. "What are you doing here?"

"Job. Going after an Atlas exec. Damien Cray. You?"

Silas's jaw tightened. "Same exec. Same data. Different client."

Tension crystallized. They were here for the same target.

"So what?" Kai asked carefully. "We fight over it?"

"Or we work together." Silas's voice was measured. "Split the data. Both get what we need."

Kai considered. "You trust us?"

"You saved my life once. Took bullets for me. I owe you." Silas extended a hand. "After this, we're square."

Kai shook it. "Deal."

Dax appeared. Saw Silas. "Holy shit. You're alive."

"Seems to be my thing lately."

Elena and Ashira joined. Crew expanded to five.

Silas gestured to his vehicle—race car, modified for speed and durability. "Three vehicles now. Better odds."

Elena pulled up tactical display. "Cray's convoy is over there. Three cars. Center one is his. Heavy security."

Kai saw them. Sleek vehicles, Atlas logos barely visible, guards with military bearing. Professional. Dangerous.

"Plan?" Ashira asked.

"Coordinate intercept," Elena said. "Overwhelm security, corner Cray, extract data, scatter before backup arrives."

"Simple," Dax muttered. "I like it."

Silas checked his chronometer. "Race starts in twenty minutes. We should position."

They moved to their vehicles. Final checks. Final preparations.

Kai took the neutralizers from his pocket. Looked at them. Three vials. Three chances to stay human if the Surge took over.

He prayed he wouldn't need them.

Loaded his pistol. Checked the mag. Racked a round.

Dax started the engine. "You ready?"

Kai looked at the staging line. Hundreds of vehicles. Thousands of people. Chaos about to begin.

Thought of Mira. Of Stitch. Of home.

"Yeah. I'm ready."

Elena's voice crackled over comms. "All units, radio check."

"Dax and Kai, good."

"Silas, copy."

"Ashira, confirmed."

The announcer's voice boomed across loudspeakers. Corpo-sponsored broadcast, illegal but unstoppable.

"RACERS TO THE LINE! CALDERA DEATH RUN BEGINS IN FIVE MINUTES!"

Crowd roared. Engines revved. Dust kicked up.

Kai's heart rate elevated. Not fear. Focus.

The Surge hummed in his spine. Dormant. Waiting.

**"COMBAT SITUATION DETECTED. STANDBY FOR ACTIVATION."**

*Not yet,* Kai thought. *Stay quiet. Just a little longer.*

**"ACKNOWLEDGED. MONITORING."**

Vehicles lined up. Elena's racing car. Dax's combat sedan. Silas's modified racer. And Damien Cray's convoy—three sleek machines, untouchable arrogance.

The announcer counted down.

"FIVE!"

Kai's hands steady on his weapon.

"FOUR!"

Dax's grip tightened on the wheel.

"THREE!"

Elena's eyes sharp, focused.

"TWO!"

Desert heat. Engine roar. Chaos about to break.

"ONE!"

Kai took a breath.

"GO!"

The world exploded into speed and dust and violence.
